    "created_at": "2016-01-19T18:04:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-01-19T18:04:14Z",
    "body": "> The spec for document.open reads like the resulting document gets its baseURI copied from the opened document\n\nWhere do you see this in the spec?\n\nWhat I see is that https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#dom-document-open step 13 removes any `<base>` tags from the document and step 21 sets the document's address to the address of the responsible document of the entry settings object (henceforth \"entry document\" to keep it short).  There's no other explicit manipulation of the base URI.  Given that and the text https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/infrastructure.html#document-base-url the base URL will be the fallback base URL, which will be either the document's address (which is that of the entry document) if that's not about:blank or something rather weird (in this case) if it is.  The about:blank part of the fallback base URL bit doesn't make much sense in this situation in some ways...\n\nThings are also a bit weird if a srcdoc document is the entry document, because copying its document address to the thing `open()` is called on is not terribly helpful.  I'm not sure anyone ever really thought through the interaction of these things, or how much it really arises in practice.\n\nAnyway, per current spec the example cited in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/536#issue-127500541 should clearly have a base URI of \"https://example.test\", since that's the document address of `ifr.contentDocument` after that `open()` call.\n\nAs for what Gecko does, it looks like we take the entry document's document address and make it the document address of the document `open` was called on.  So far that's per spec.  We _also_ take the document base URL of the entry document at the moment in time when `open` is called and store it in the \"baseURL\" field (as described in https://github.com/whatwg/html/issues/421#issuecomment-169565696) of the document `open` is called on.  Effectively, this snapshots the base URL of the entry document and forces the result onto the document `open` is called on.  This forcing can later be overridden via adding/removing `<base>` elements.  In practice, I expect pretty much no one writing out documents via `open` is later _removing_ `<base>` elements from them, which is why I expect we have gotten away with this wart.\n\nGecko's approach does have the benefit of working sanely when the entry document is a srcdoc document or an about:blank document, since it goes to the trouble of extracting their actual document base URL, which does all the complicated \"walk up to the parent, etc\" work.  At the same time it has the benefit of not having to keep track of where `open` was called from and not having to do later base URL lookups dynamically.  It has the potential drawback that if the entry document's document base URL changes the target of `open` does not experience a corresponding change, and the drawback that the interaction with dynamic _removal_ of `<base>` elements is weird.\n\nI would be quite interested in what IE does here, since Gecko basically reverse-engineered this stuff from IE for compat reasons as I recall...\n",
