    "body": "> How about the base URL\n\nRight, see discussion above.\n\n> origin\n\nThis one is interesting.  Clearly all the things involved have the same effective script origin, but it's not clear what should happen with the origin.\n\nI believe what Gecko does here is that it will throw if the entry document is not same-origin (in addition to being same-effective-script-origin) with the document `open` is called on, and then keeping the same origin we used to have, which is the only origin around at that point.\n\n> and it's using the Document associated with the global object (a Window)\n\nAs the spec is written right this second, it's using the document associated with the global the `open` function came from, since it's using the \"realm execution context\".  I don't think that's a sane thing to do here.  @domenic, thoughts?\n\n> Is that necessarily the same as the entry settings object's responsible document?\n\nNot at all.\n",
